import fs from 'node:fs';
const c=(title,kind,lines,script)=>({title,formula:lines[0],points:lines,board:{kind,lines},script});
const chapters=[
c('Flower structure and the anther','anther',['Stamen: filament and anther','Carpel: stigma, style and ovary','Typical young anther has four microsporangia','Tapetum supports developing pollen'],`Welcome to Reproduction, our NEET Biology Unit six foundation. This recording uses an AI generated Marin voice. A flower organizes structures involved in sexual reproduction. Stamens contain a filament and anther, while the gynoecium includes carpels with stigma, style and ovary. Ovules occur inside the ovary in flowering plants. A typical young angiosperm anther is bilobed and contains four microsporangia, which develop into pollen sacs. Their wall layers include epidermis, endothecium, middle layers and the innermost tapetum in the elementary account. The tapetum supports developing microspores and contributes materials important for pollen development. Sporogenous cells inside give rise to microspore mother cells. The whole anther and its wall tissues belong to the diploid sporophyte; this does not make every later cell inside it diploid. Keep organ, tissue and cell stages separate when assigning ploidy. This class follows plant reproduction, human reproduction and reproductive health, with further detailed diagrams and mixed applications remaining.`),
c('Microsporogenesis and the male gametophyte','pollen',['Microspore mother cell undergoes meiosis','Four haploid microspores form a tetrad','Pollen contains vegetative and generative lineages','Generative cell forms two male gametes'],`Microsporogenesis is the formation of haploid microspores from a diploid microspore mother cell through meiosis. A typical meiotic event produces four microspores in a tetrad. Subsequent development of a microspore produces the male gametophyte, so microspore formation and gametophyte development are different stages. A pollen grain commonly has an outer exine containing resistant sporopollenin and an inner intine. Germ pores provide regions associated with tube emergence. In the familiar two celled pollen stage, a large vegetative cell accompanies a generative cell. The generative lineage divides mitotically to form two male gametes, either before pollen release in some species or later in others. Thus not every mature pollen grain must be shed at the same cell number. The vegetative cell supports pollen tube growth; it is not a third male gamete. Distinguish a haploid microspore, a pollen grain containing several cells and each individual sperm cell when interpreting counts and developmental sequences.`),
c('Megasporogenesis and the embryo sac','embryo-sac',['Megaspore mother cell undergoes meiosis','Common monosporic pattern retains one megaspore','Typical mature sac: seven cells, eight nuclei','Egg and synergids occur at the micropylar end'],`Inside an ovule, the nucellus contains a megaspore mother cell that typically undergoes meiosis to produce four haploid megaspores. In the familiar monosporic Polygonum type account, one functional megaspore contributes to the female gametophyte while the other three degenerate. Three mitotic nuclear divisions lead to an eight nucleate embryo sac, followed by the characteristic cellular organization. The egg apparatus at the micropylar end contains one egg and two synergids, whose filiform apparatus helps guide and interact with the pollen tube. Three antipodal cells occur toward the chalazal end. The large central cell contains two polar nuclei, which may fuse before fertilization. This gives the standard description of seven cells and eight nuclei before polar nuclear fusion. The whole ovule is not the embryo sac: integuments and nucellus are sporophytic tissues around it. Other developmental patterns exist, so use the stated typical pattern rather than assuming every flowering plant has an identical female gametophyte.`),
c('Pollination types and genetic relationships','pollination',['Autogamy: within the same flower','Geitonogamy: between flowers on one plant','Xenogamy: between different plants','Pollination transfers pollen, not a zygote'],`Pollination in angiosperms is transfer of pollen from anther to stigma. It precedes fertilization and is not itself fusion of gametes. Autogamy occurs within the same flower. Geitonogamy transfers pollen between different flowers on the same plant; it can require a pollinating agent but remains genetically self pollination in the usual comparison. Xenogamy involves flowers on different individual plants of the same species and can introduce a different parental genotype. A bisexual flower does not automatically self pollinate because timing, spatial arrangement and compatibility can prevent it. Cleistogamous flowers remain closed and favor assured self pollination, while chasmogamous flowers open and expose their reproductive structures. Whether a transfer is mechanically between two flowers and whether it introduces genetic differences are separate questions. A stigma receiving pollen does not guarantee that it is compatible or that a pollen tube will successfully reach an ovule. Track the source plant, source flower and destination before assigning the pollination category.`),
c('Pollinating agents and representative examples','agents',['Wind-pollinated flowers often expose anthers','Many animal-pollinated flowers offer rewards','Vallisneria: pollination near the water surface','Zostera: underwater pollen transport'],`Pollinating agents include wind, water and animals, with floral traits associated with the relevant transfer route. Wind pollinated flowers commonly have exposed anthers, abundant light pollen and receptive structures suited to interception. Such tendencies are not definitions based on flower color alone. Many animal pollinated flowers use scent, visual signals or rewards such as nectar, while some involve specialized interactions. Water pollination is uncommon among flowering plants and should not be assigned to every aquatic species. Vallisneria provides the familiar surface water comparison, while Zostera illustrates underwater transport with elongated pollen suited to that setting. Water lilies and water hyacinth do not establish hydrophily merely because the plants grow in water. Pollen traits, stigma arrangement and the actual transport mechanism must be considered together. A pollinator moving between flowers may carry pollen without every visit producing fertilization. Distinguish habitat, attracting an animal, transferring pollen and completing reproduction when interpreting an example.`),
c('Outbreeding devices and controlled pollination','outbreeding',['Different timing or positions can limit selfing','Self-incompatibility rejects suitable self pollen','Dioecy prevents autogamy and geitonogamy','Emasculation and bagging have different roles'],`Flowering plants can reduce self pollination through several mechanisms. Different timing of pollen release and stigma receptivity can limit selfing, while spatial separation of anthers and stigma can reduce contact. Self incompatibility involves recognition processes that prevent successful self pollen performance in the relevant system; it is not simply failure to produce any pollen. Monoecious plants have separate male and female flowers on one individual, preventing autogamy within a unisexual flower but not necessarily geitonogamy. Dioecious plants separate these flower types onto different individuals, preventing both routes of selfing in the usual account. In controlled breeding, emasculation removes anthers from an appropriate bisexual flower before pollen release, while bagging limits unwanted pollen access. The desired compatible pollen can then be applied under the planned procedure. A female unisexual flower does not require anther removal when it has no anthers. Keep prevention of pollen contact separate from genetic compatibility and from deliberate selection of the pollen source.`),
c('Pollen-pistil interaction and tube growth','pollen-tube',['Stigma and pollen interact before fertilization','Compatible pollen can germinate and grow a tube','Tube transports two male gametes toward embryo sac','Micropylar entry is the common teaching pattern'],`Pollen pistil interaction includes events from pollen deposition through recognition, germination and tube growth toward the female gametophyte. Compatible pollen can germinate on the stigma, and the tube grows through the relevant pistil tissues toward an ovule. The pistil is not a passive hollow pipe accepting every pollen grain equally. In the common teaching account, a pollen tube enters through the micropyle and reaches the embryo sac through a synergid associated with the filiform apparatus. It releases two male gametes, which participate in the two fusion events of double fertilization. The pollen tube is a growing cellular extension, not a miniature seed or an independently swimming embryo. A tube can carry male gametes formed before or after pollen release, depending on the species. Arrival at the stigma and arrival inside the embryo sac are separate stages with possible barriers between them. Use compatibility, route and developmental stage together when deciding what a pollen tube has actually accomplished.`),
c('Double fertilization and ploidy','double',['One sperm plus egg forms the diploid zygote','Other sperm joins the central-cell nuclei','Typical primary endosperm nucleus is triploid','Syngamy plus triple fusion = double fertilization'],`Angiosperm double fertilization contains two distinct fusion events. One male gamete fuses with the egg, producing the diploid zygote through syngamy. The other fuses with the central cell's two polar nuclei, or their combined secondary nucleus, producing the primary endosperm nucleus. In the typical diploid plant and standard embryo sac pattern, the endosperm nucleus is triploid because three haploid nuclear contributions combine. Triple fusion describes the three nuclear contributions, not three separate sperm cells. Together, syngamy and this central cell fertilization constitute double fertilization. Only one of the two male gametes forms the embryo's zygotic starting cell. The surrounding integuments remain maternal sporophytic tissue and are not made triploid by proximity. Ploidy calculations must state the parental and developmental pattern because unusual embryo sac systems can differ. Do not confuse the number of fusion events, number of gametes delivered and number of chromosome sets in each resulting structure.`),
c('Endosperm and embryo development','embryo',['Endosperm supports embryo nutrition','Nuclear and cellular development patterns differ','Dicot embryo passes globular and heart stages','Cotyledons, radicle and plumule have distinct roles'],`After fertilization, endosperm development supplies an important nutritive context for the embryo in many flowering plants. Nuclear endosperm development begins with nuclear divisions without immediate walls between all nuclei, whereas cellular development couples division with cellular partitioning more directly. Coconut provides a familiar comparison: liquid endosperm contains a free nuclear component, while the kernel represents cellular endosperm tissue. The zygote develops into an embryo through organized divisions and differentiation. In a typical dicot account, globular and heart shaped stages precede the mature embryo with two cotyledons and an embryonic axis. The radicle contributes to the root system and the plumule to the shoot. A monocot embryo has one cotyledon, with specialized protective structures in familiar grass examples. Endosperm and embryo are different developmental products even when both occur in one seed. Not every mature seed retains abundant endosperm because reserves may be transferred or used during development. Distinguish tissue origin, nutritional role and final seed contents.`),
c('Seed and fruit formation and significance','seed-fruit',['Ovule generally becomes seed; ovary becomes fruit','Integuments contribute the seed coat','Seed protects and helps disperse the embryo','Fruit can include substantial accessory tissues'],`In the usual post fertilization sequence, an ovule develops into a seed and the ovary develops into a fruit. Integuments contribute the seed coat, while the ovary wall contributes the pericarp. The embryo and retained nutritive tissues have different origins from these maternal coverings. Some fruits, such as apple in the familiar comparison, include a substantial contribution from tissues outside the ovary, so fruit formation should not be reduced to one identical anatomical pattern in every species. Seeds protect embryos, package reserves and can support dispersal and survival through unfavorable conditions. Dormancy can separate seed maturation from immediate germination, but not every species has the same dormancy requirements. Fruit traits can influence dispersal by wind, water or animals in suitable examples. A seed is not simply an ovule containing an unchanged egg forever. Connect fertilization products, protective tissues, maturation and dispersal while keeping the special cases in the next chapter separate from the ordinary sequence.`),
c('Apomixis, parthenocarpy and polyembryony','special',['Apomixis: seed reproduction without usual fertilization','Parthenocarpy: fruit develops without fertilization','Polyembryony: more than one embryo in a seed','Endosperm requirements can differ in apomicts'],`Apomixis concerns seed reproduction without the usual sexual fertilization route to the embryo. Mechanisms can include an unreduced embryo sac or embryo formation from maternal sporophytic tissues, depending on the plant. This can preserve a parental genotype and has relevance to maintaining useful hybrid combinations, but mechanisms and genetic outcomes should not be assumed identical in every species. In some apomictic systems, endosperm formation can still require a fertilization related event, so apomixis must not automatically be defined as absence of every possible fusion anywhere in the seed. Parthenocarpy is different: a fruit develops without fertilization. It concerns fruit development, not necessarily formation of a clonal embryo. Polyembryony means more than one embryo occurs within one seed; Citrus and some mango examples are familiar. Those embryos can arise through different routes rather than all being independent sexual zygotes. Distinguish embryo origin, fruit development and embryo number before matching one of these three terms.`),
c('Human male system and testis microanatomy','testis',['Seminiferous tubules support sperm production','Sertoli cells support developing germ cells','Leydig cells produce testicular androgens','Ducts and accessory glands have different roles'],`The human male reproductive system includes testes, duct pathways, accessory glands and external structures. Testes contain numerous seminiferous tubules within lobules. Germ cells develop in association with supporting Sertoli cells, while interstitial Leydig cells produce androgens such as testosterone. The scrotal arrangement helps maintain a temperature favorable for ordinary sperm production, rather than meaning every testicular process needs an identical fixed temperature in all circumstances. Sperm pass from testicular pathways toward the epididymis and through the vas deferens and subsequent duct route. The epididymis contributes to maturation and storage functions. Seminal vesicles, prostate and bulbourethral glands add secretions with different contributions to seminal fluid. Semen is therefore not simply pure sperm cells with no fluid component. Sperm production, maturation, transport and accessory gland secretion are distinct processes. For microscopic questions, identify the seminiferous epithelium, its supporting cells and the interstitial compartment instead of assigning every cell in the testis the same hormone or gamete producing role.`),
c('Human female system and ovary microanatomy','ovary',['Ovary contains follicles at different stages','Oviduct has infundibular, ampullary and isthmic regions','Uterine endometrium supports implantation','Urinary and reproductive openings are distinct'],`The human female reproductive system includes ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, cervix and vagina, with associated external structures and mammary glands. The ovaries contain follicles at different developmental stages, with oocytes surrounded by supporting follicular cells. As follicles grow, granulosa and thecal organization and a fluid filled antrum become important in the familiar mature follicle account. The uterine tube has a funnel shaped infundibular region with fimbriae, an ampullary region and a narrower isthmus leading toward the uterus. It does not form a closed pipe directly fused to the ovary. The uterine wall includes an inner endometrium and muscular myometrium with different roles. The cervix connects the uterine cavity with the vaginal canal through its passage. The urethra belongs to the urinary system and has a separate opening; it is not a reproductive duct. Distinguish an ovarian follicle, the cell it contains and the tissues later supporting an implanted embryo.`),
c('Spermatogenesis, spermiogenesis and spermiation','sperm',['Primary spermatocyte undergoes meiosis I','Secondary spermatocytes undergo meiosis II','One primary cell can yield four spermatids','Maturation and release are different from meiosis'],`Spermatogenesis includes the production and development of sperm from germ cell lineages in the seminiferous tubules. Spermatogonia proliferate through mitosis, and selected cells enter the meiotic sequence as primary spermatocytes. Following DNA replication, a primary spermatocyte is diploid with duplicated chromosomes. Meiosis one produces haploid secondary spermatocytes whose chromosomes still have sister chromatids. Meiosis two separates the sisters to produce haploid spermatids. The usual yield is four spermatids from one primary spermatocyte, not four fully separate diploid gametes. Spermiogenesis is the transformation of spermatids into the specialized sperm form, including nuclear condensation, acrosomal organization and flagellar development. Spermiation is release from the supporting epithelium into the tubule lumen. Later maturation functions also occur along the duct pathway. Hypothalamic GnRH, pituitary gonadotropins and testicular signals coordinate the process. LH supports Leydig cell androgen production, while FSH and androgen actions support the relevant Sertoli and germ cell environment.`),
c('Oogenesis and meiotic arrest','oocyte',['Primary oocytes begin meiosis before birth','Prophase I arrest can persist for years','Ovulated secondary oocyte is in metaphase II','Fertilization normally triggers meiotic completion'],`Human oogenesis differs from sperm production in timing, division pattern and cytoplasmic allocation. Primary oocytes enter meiosis during fetal development and arrest in prophase one. After puberty, selected follicles develop during cycles, and a primary oocyte completing meiosis one produces a large secondary oocyte and a small polar body through unequal division. The secondary oocyte enters meiosis two and normally arrests at metaphase two before ovulation. It is therefore not correct to label the ordinarily ovulated cell as an egg that has already completed every meiotic division. Fertilization related activation normally triggers completion of meiosis two and another polar body. The division pattern preserves most cytoplasm in the functional female gamete instead of producing four equivalent large eggs. Chromosome set number and DNA content must be distinguished: a haploid secondary oocyte still has duplicated chromosomes before sister separation. Follicle growth, oocyte meiosis and ovulation are coordinated but are not three names for the same event.`),
c('Ovarian and menstrual cycles','cycle',['Follicular development and endometrial change interact','Sustained high estrogen can trigger positive feedback','LH surge is associated with ovulation','Corpus luteum supports the secretory phase'],`The ovarian and uterine cycles are coordinated by hypothalamic, pituitary and ovarian signaling. During the follicular phase, developing follicles produce estrogen and the endometrium proliferates in the familiar account. Sustained sufficiently high estrogen near the preovulatory stage can produce positive feedback associated with the LH surge, which helps trigger ovulation. After ovulation, the corpus luteum produces progesterone and other signals that support a secretory endometrium. If pregnancy related support does not occur, luteal regression and falling hormone levels contribute to menstrual shedding of the functional endometrial layer. Menstruation is not shedding of the entire uterus or proof that every ovarian cell has died. A twenty eight day cycle with ovulation near the middle is a teaching model, not a universal timetable or a reliable prediction for every person. Menarche begins menstrual cycling and menopause marks its eventual cessation in the relevant life stage. Relate hormone graphs to both ovarian and uterine events.`),
c('Human fertilization and the sperm-oocyte interaction','fertilization',['Fertilization usually occurs in the uterine tube','Textbook location: ampullary-isthmic region','Sperm preparation and acrosomal events aid interaction','Fusion triggers responses limiting polyspermy'],`Human fertilization normally occurs in the uterine tube, described near the ampullary isthmic region in the familiar NEET textbook account. It is not ordinarily an event inside the uterine endometrium. Sperm undergo functional changes in the reproductive tract, including capacitation, that support fertilizing ability. Interactions with the oocyte coverings and acrosomal events help the appropriate sperm reach and fuse with the oocyte membrane. Fusion triggers oocyte activation, completion of meiosis two and responses that limit additional sperm entry. The male and female nuclear contributions establish the diploid genetic complement of the zygote in the usual account. Delivery of many sperm does not mean the normal embryo receives many paternal chromosome sets. The acrosome and sperm nucleus have different roles, while the flagellum supports movement rather than supplying the entire embryonic body. Fertilization, gamete transport and later implantation occur at different locations and developmental times, so trace them separately on a reproductive tract diagram.`),
c('Cleavage, morula and blastocyst','blastocyst',['Cleavage increases cell number without major growth','Blastomeres become a compact morula','Blastocyst has cavity, outer layer and inner cell mass','Trophoblast and inner cell mass have different fates'],`After fertilization, the zygote undergoes cleavage divisions as it moves toward the uterus. These early mitotic divisions increase the number of blastomeres without a corresponding large increase in the overall size of the conceptus. Individual cells therefore become smaller in the elementary comparison. A compact solid cell mass is described as a morula, followed by formation of a cavity and organization of the blastocyst. The blastocyst has an outer trophoblast, an inner cell mass and the blastocoel cavity. The inner cell mass contributes to the embryo proper, while the trophoblast contributes importantly to implantation related and extraembryonic structures. The outer layer should not be described as simply becoming the entire fetus. Cleavage is mitotic, not repeated meiosis that halves chromosome number at every division. The protective zona pellucida is shed before normal implantation proceeds. Sequence, cellular arrangement and location are more useful than treating one exact day or one cell count as universal for every developing embryo.`),
c('Implantation and placental organization','implantation',['Blastocyst attaches to receptive endometrium','Trophoblast contributes to chorionic structures','Fetal and maternal tissues form the interface','Exchange does not imply unrestricted blood mixing'],`Implantation involves attachment and embedding of the blastocyst in receptive uterine endometrium. It follows fertilization and cleavage rather than being another name for sperm egg fusion. Trophoblast differentiation and interaction with maternal tissues contribute to the developing placental interface, with chorionic villi and maternal endometrial contributions in the elementary account. Maternal and fetal circulations approach one another across an exchange interface; they do not ordinarily become one freely mixing pool of blood. Oxygen, nutrients and wastes can move through suitable transfer mechanisms, but the placenta is not an absolute barrier to every drug, infectious agent or other substance. A normal uterine implantation site must be distinguished from ectopic implantation outside the usual uterine location. The structures have different maternal and embryonic origins even when they cooperate as one functional exchange organ. Trace which circulation supplies a substance, what interface it crosses and which circulation receives it before interpreting a placental diagram.`),
c('Pregnancy and placental hormones','pregnancy',['Early hCG supports the corpus luteum','Placenta contributes progesterone and estrogens','hPL contributes to maternal metabolic adaptation','Pregnancy dating conventions must be specified'],`Pregnancy is sustained by coordinated embryonic, placental and maternal changes. Early human chorionic gonadotropin, or hCG, supports corpus luteum function and continued hormone production in the familiar sequence. As pregnancy progresses, the placenta becomes an important source of progesterone and estrogens and produces other signals including human placental lactogen. These hormones participate in maintaining the uterine environment and adapting maternal physiology. An early hormonal signal is not itself a miniature embryo, and a hormone test does not describe every aspect of embryonic development. Placental exchange and endocrine functions are related but distinct roles. Developmental age counted from fertilization differs from conventional obstetric gestational age counted from the last menstrual period; approximate timelines should always state the reference. This class gives an elementary pregnancy account rather than a detailed clinical schedule. Maternal tissues, extraembryonic structures and the embryo remain distinct compartments even as their functions become closely coordinated over time.`),
c('Parturition and positive feedback','parturition',['Fetal and maternal signals contribute to labor onset','Cervical stretch supports neuroendocrine signaling','Oxytocin strengthens suitable uterine contractions','Positive feedback differs from ordinary negative loops'],`Parturition is the process of birth and involves coordinated uterine, cervical, fetal and maternal events. In the elementary neuroendocrine account, signals associated with the mature fetus and placenta contribute to the onset of the process. Cervical stretch and uterine activity support feedback that increases oxytocin release and strengthens appropriate myometrial contractions. Stronger contractions can create further stretch, illustrating a positive feedback loop that continues toward delivery. Prostaglandins and other local and systemic factors also participate, so oxytocin should not be presented as the only molecule involved. This positive feedback example contrasts with the stabilizing negative feedback common in many endocrine axes. The uterine muscle is the contracting myometrium, not an entire ovary pushing the fetus directly into the birth canal. The account explains biological coordination and is not a procedural guide to labor management. Keep the triggering signal, neural pathway, hormone release and mechanical response separate when ordering the feedback events.`),
c('Lactation and passive immune support','lactation',['Prolactin supports milk production','Oxytocin supports milk ejection','Colostrum contains protective immune components','Passive antibody transfer differs from vaccination'],`Mammary glands undergo changes during pregnancy and support milk secretion after birth under appropriate endocrine conditions. Prolactin supports milk production, while oxytocin promotes contraction of myoepithelial cells and milk ejection. These are different responses, so a question about formation of milk should not automatically be answered with the hormone chiefly associated with its release. Suckling related sensory input can participate in neuroendocrine feedback supporting both processes. Colostrum, the early secretion, contains immune components including antibodies that contribute to passive protection of the infant. Passive transfer supplies existing protective molecules; it is different from the infant generating its own memory response after vaccination. Milk also provides nutrients, but immune protection should not be described as a guarantee that no infection can occur. The physiological account concerns glandular tissue, hormone signaling and transfer of materials rather than one hormone replacing every aspect of infant nutrition. Relate each named signal to its target cells and immediate functional effect.`),
c('Reproductive health and sexually transmitted infections','sti',['STIs can be present without obvious symptoms','Bacterial, viral and other pathogens differ','Condoms reduce risk but do not eliminate every risk','Vaccines prevent some infections, not all STIs'],`Reproductive health includes informed understanding of reproductive functions, prevention of disease and access to appropriate care without stigma. Sexually transmitted infections can be caused by different pathogens, including bacteria, viruses and other organisms, and many infections can be present without obvious symptoms. Gonorrhoea, syphilis, chlamydial infection, HIV infection, genital herpes and HPV provide familiar examples with different biological consequences. Some infections can also spread through routes such as blood exposure or from parent to infant; not every transmission event has the same route. Correct and consistent condom use reduces many sexual transmission risks but does not eliminate every risk, especially where exposed skin can transmit infection. Vaccines are available against infections including hepatitis B and HPV, not against every STI. Testing and pathogen appropriate professional care matter because symptoms alone cannot reliably identify or exclude an infection. These are general prevention principles for study, not a diagnosis or a drug regimen. Avoid assigning blame to a person based on an infection label.`),
c('Contraceptive categories: barriers and behavioral methods','barriers',['Contraception aims to prevent pregnancy','Barriers limit sperm access to the reproductive tract','Calendar methods depend on variable cycle timing','Lactational amenorrhea has restrictive conditions'],`Contraception includes methods intended to prevent pregnancy through different biological mechanisms. Barrier methods such as condoms limit sperm entry or passage, while diaphragms and related devices cover the cervical region in suitable use. Condoms also reduce many STI transmission risks, a property not shared by every contraceptive category. Behavioral approaches include avoiding intercourse during estimated fertile intervals and withdrawal, but their practical effectiveness depends on behavior and biological variability. A simple calendar diagram cannot establish a universally safe day because ovulation timing varies. Lactational amenorrhea is associated with suppression of reproductive cycling under restricted conditions; it is not a guarantee that any breastfeeding person cannot conceive. A method's mechanism, typical effectiveness, reversibility and infection protection are different properties. Choosing a method involves individual circumstances and appropriate healthcare information rather than selecting whichever name appears first in a textbook table. For examination comparisons, identify what stage is being interrupted and do not equate prevention of fertilization with termination of an established pregnancy.`),
c('Hormonal contraception, IUDs and emergency methods','contraception',['Hormonal methods can alter ovulation and cervical mucus','Copper IUDs impair sperm function','Hormonal IUDs act through local and other effects','Emergency contraception does not terminate pregnancy'],`Hormonal contraceptives act through combinations of effects that can include suppression of ovulation and changes in cervical mucus and the reproductive environment. Preparations differ, so one exact mechanism should not be assigned identically to every product. Intrauterine devices also differ: copper containing devices impair sperm function and fertilization, while hormone releasing devices commonly thicken cervical mucus and create other local effects, with ovulation responses depending on the method and context. It is misleading to describe every IUD as acting only after implantation. Emergency contraception aims to prevent pregnancy after a relevant exposure, for example through delaying or preventing ovulation for suitable pill methods. It does not terminate an established pregnancy and should not be confused with medical termination. This recording compares mechanisms rather than giving dosing, timing or product selection instructions. Effectiveness, eligibility and appropriate use require method specific information. In a question, distinguish the active component, its target process and whether infection protection is also provided.`),
c('Sterilization and duct interruption','sterilization',['Vasectomy interrupts sperm transport in vas deferens','Tubal procedures interrupt gamete encounter routes','Gonads are not necessarily removed','Sterilization does not itself prevent STI transmission'],`Sterilization methods aim at long term prevention of pregnancy by interrupting reproductive pathways. In vasectomy, the vas deferens pathway is interrupted so sperm do not enter the ejaculatory route in the usual way after the method has become effective. The testes are not removed, and testosterone production and accessory gland secretion do not automatically cease. Tubal sterilization interrupts the uterine tube pathway, limiting the encounter and transport processes required for ordinary fertilization. It is not removal of the ovaries or the whole uterus, and ovarian hormone production does not simply stop because a tube is blocked. These methods are considered permanent contraception in standard counseling, even though technical reversal may sometimes be attempted without guaranteed success. They do not themselves provide STI protection. The classroom account explains anatomy and mechanism, not a procedural instruction for performing surgery. Separate the organ producing gametes, the duct transporting them and the hormones released into blood when predicting the effects of a duct interruption.`),
c('MTP, amniocentesis and regulated diagnostic use','diagnostics',['MTP ends a pregnancy; contraception prevents one','Amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid for indicated tests','A diagnostic sample does not reveal every condition','India prohibits sex selection and regulated misuse'],`Medical termination of pregnancy concerns ending a pregnancy, whereas contraception aims to prevent one. MTP is a regulated healthcare matter involving clinical and legal requirements; a brief biological lesson is not a statement of personal eligibility or a complete current legal procedure. Amniocentesis involves obtaining amniotic fluid for appropriate diagnostic investigation, including analysis of fetal cells or relevant substances in indicated contexts. It is not a routine guarantee that every possible disorder can be detected or excluded. Diagnostic testing, screening and sex selection are different purposes. In India, the PCPNDT framework prohibits sex selection and communication of fetal sex in the regulated prenatal diagnostic context, while allowing specified legitimate diagnostic uses subject to its requirements. This distinction prevents the misleading claim that every prenatal diagnostic test is prohibited, or that scientific ability automatically makes any use lawful. The examination focus is the biological purpose and prevention of misuse. Always separate the sample obtained, the information sought and the conditions governing its use.`),
c('Infertility and IVF with embryo transfer','ivf',['Infertility can involve factors in either partner','IVF: fertilization outside the body','Embryo transfer follows laboratory development','Gametes, zygotes and embryos are distinct stages'],`Infertility can involve factors affecting sperm production or transport, oocyte development, tubal function, implantation and other processes in either partner or both. It should not automatically be attributed to the female partner. Assisted reproductive technologies address selected problems through different methods rather than one universal solution. In vitro fertilization, or IVF, involves bringing gametes together for fertilization outside the body under laboratory conditions, followed by appropriate embryo development and transfer. Embryo transfer is therefore not another name for the initial meeting of gametes. A transferred embryo is not a completely developed fetus. Intracytoplasmic sperm injection is a related laboratory fertilization technique in which a sperm is introduced into an oocyte, differing from ordinary co incubation of gametes. Artificial insemination instead introduces sperm into the reproductive tract and is not itself embryo transfer. These are elementary distinctions rather than recommendations about which treatment a particular person needs. Match the biological stage, site of fertilization and material transferred before assigning a technique name.`),
c('ZIFT, GIFT and transfer-stage comparisons','art',['ZIFT transfers a zygote or early embryo into a tube','GIFT transfers gametes, with fertilization in vivo','IUT denotes transfer into the uterus','Textbook stage comparisons differ from treatment selection'],`Zygote intrafallopian transfer, or ZIFT, describes transfer of a zygote or suitable early embryo into a uterine tube after fertilization has occurred outside the body. In the familiar NCERT comparison, zygotes or embryos up to eight blastomeres are associated with this early transfer category, while later embryos are described with intrauterine transfer. These are stated textbook stage comparisons, not a universal current clinical protocol. Gamete intrafallopian transfer, or GIFT, instead transfers gametes into the tube so that fertilization can occur within the body. The transferred material is therefore different even when the destination includes a tube in both names. Intrauterine transfer specifies a uterine destination, not necessarily the same stage as gamete transfer. Laboratory and clinical choices depend on circumstances and available practice, and some named techniques are now less commonly used. For an examination diagram, identify whether fertilization has already happened, what is being transferred and where it is placed. Those three questions distinguish the methods more reliably than the abbreviations alone.`),
c('Integrated reproduction and developmental accounting','integration',['Trace meiosis, mitosis and fusion separately','Name the tissue before assigning ploidy','Separate reproductive stage from healthcare method','Further diagrams, counts and mixed cases remain'],`Reproduction questions often combine a developmental sequence with a count, a location or a named technique. Begin by identifying the organism, tissue and starting cell. Meiosis reduces chromosome set number, mitosis usually preserves it, and fusion combines nuclear contributions. The typical plant endosperm example and a human zygote therefore require different accounting even though both follow fertilization related events. Distinguish pollination from gamete fusion, ovulation from meiotic completion and implantation from fertilization. For reproductive health methods, identify the target process and avoid assuming that pregnancy prevention, infection protection and diagnostic testing are identical functions. This recorded Unit six foundation covers flowering plant reproduction, human reproductive systems and development, and the prescribed reproductive health headings against the dated official syllabus. It provides original practice questions and teaching boards rather than official past examination items. Further labelled diagrams, ploidy counts, hormonal graphs and mixed experimental or clinical interpretation remain. Continue by explaining the sequence and its assumptions before choosing a memorized label.`)
];
